<InfoPage title="Website privacy" description="What the public wrenlore.com marketing site collects, and what belongs to a separate self-hosted WrenLore deployment." updated="25 November 2026">
  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>wrenlore.com is a static website served over HTTPS. It does not provide user accounts, accept document uploads or run advertising trackers. The current site does not set analytics or marketing cookies.</p>
  <p>Like most web servers, the hosting system may record ordinary request logs such as IP address, timestamp, requested path, response status and user-agent string. These logs are used for security and operational troubleshooting. WrenLore also derives aggregate counts for website page views, installer-script requests and public demo visits from those logs. The aggregate report retains counts, dates and response classes, not IP addresses or user-agent strings.</p>
  <p>There is no advertising tracker, cross-site identifier or hidden installation-success callback. An installer-script request or container pull shows distribution interest; it does not expose the content, users or success state of a self-hosted deployment.</p>

  <h2>Links to other services</h2>
  <p>The site links to GitHub and other external documentation. Those services process requests under their own privacy policies. Following an external link leaves wrenlore.com.</p>

  <h2>Self-hosted deployments</h2>
  <p>A WrenLore application installed by another organisation is operated by that organisation. Its users, pages, files, audit records, identity configuration and model-provider choices are controlled by that deployment operator. This website does not receive that deployment’s wiki content merely because it runs WrenLore.</p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>For questions about this public site, open an issue in the <a href="https://github.com/wrenlore/wrenlore-website/issues">website repository</a>. For a specific WrenLore installation, contact its operator.</p>
</InfoPage>
